  "transcript": "User: Hi, I saw your ad about missed calls. We lose calls after hours and I'm curious how it works.\nAgent: Got it — thanks for clicking the ad and sharing that. After you close for the day, when a call comes in now, does it usually go to voicemail, or what actually happens to that caller?\nUser: After hours, calls usually go to voicemail.\nAgent: Mhm, makes sense — that’s what most people tell me. Those after-hours voicemails… do people actually leave messages, or do they tend to hang up and call someone else?\nUser: Most hang up, but about 15 missed calls a week go to voicemail.\nAgent: Got it — that’s helpful. Has anyone ever walked you through what those 15 missed calls a week are actually costing you, or should we do a quick back-of-the-napkin math on it?\nUser: No one has walked me through it, but I'd be interested in doing the math with you.\nAgent: Gotcha — that helps. So of those 15 missed calls a week, what percentage do you think would’ve actually booked with you if someone picked up… maybe 50%, 60%?\nUser: About 60% would book if someone answered.\nAgent: Got it, that’s helpful. And ballpark, what’s your average job or ticket worth when someone does book — are we talking more like $200, $500, $1,000 per job?\nUser: The average job value is about $450.\nAgent: Alright, so with your numbers, those missed after-hours calls are costing you about $17,500 a month in work you never even see. So… to your original question, do you want the simple version of how we stop that leakage, or a bit more detail?\nUser: I'd like to know the simple version of how you stop that leakage, please.\nAgent: Makes sense. So, simple version: when you can’t pick up — especially after hours — our system answers like a real receptionist, has a quick conversation, and either books the job, sends you the details, or captures a proper message so that caller doesn’t disappear to your competitor.  \n\nGiven those numbers we walked through, would you be open to a 20‑minute